  "answer": "I'll look through how product special prices are stored and how the storefront API decides to show a discount.The storefront price check treats an end date as if the special has not started, and the admin product mapper saves that end date into the start-date field. I'll lock that in with a test, then fix both.Special prices that include an end date never reached the storefront as a discount. The admin product mapper wrote that end date into the special start-date field, and the catalogue price calculation then required the start date to be both before and after today, so the discount was dropped. The end date is now stored on the special end date, and an active window is start-before-today and end-after-today. A unit test covers a special with both dates, one with only an end date, and one with no dates.\n\nVERIFICATION: {\"ran\": [\"mvn -pl sm-core -am -Dtest=ProductPriceUtilsSpecialPriceTest -DfailIfNoTests=false test\"], \"result\": \"passed\", \"could_not_verify\": [\"admin REST create path and live storefront HTTP; the mapper change was not executed in a test\"], \"missing\": [\"a unit test or API fixture that posts a product with discountEndDate and reads it back\"]}",
